### Describe the bug

With output caching locking enabled (the default), concurrent requests for the same cache key wait on the first request via `ScheduleAsync` in `OutputCacheMiddleware`. If the client of that first request disconnects and the endpoint observes `HttpContext.RequestAborted` (the recommended pattern), the resulting `OperationCanceledException` is propagated to every waiting request (`DispatcherExtensions.ScheduleAsync` calls `tcs.SetException(ex)`), and `OutputCacheMiddleware` doesn't handle it. So unrelated, still-connected clients get a 500 because a different client went away.

Related but different: #56427 / #66877 (truncated body of an aborted request being cached).

### Expected Behavior

The waiting request isn't canceled itself, so it should run the endpoint on its own (or become the new leader) and return 200. The cache lookup path already treats `OperationCanceledException` from its dispatcher as a miss (`OutputCacheMiddleware.cs`, "don't report as failure").

### Steps To Reproduce

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddOutputCache();
builder.WebHost.UseUrls("http://127.0.0.1:5077");
var app = builder.Build();
app.UseOutputCache();
var n = 0;
app.MapGet("/slow", async (HttpContext ctx) =>
{
    var i = Interlocked.Increment(ref n);
    await Task.Delay(1500, ctx.RequestAborted);
    return $"value {i}";
}).CacheOutput();
await app.StartAsync();

using var http = new HttpClient { BaseAddress = new Uri("http://127.0.0.1:5077") };
using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
var leader = http.GetAsync("/slow", cts.Token);
await Task.Delay(200);
var follower = http.GetAsync("/slow");   // same cache key, coalesced behind the leader
await Task.Delay(300);
cts.Cancel();                            // leader's client goes away
try { await leader; } catch { }
var r = await follower;
Console.WriteLine($"follower: {(int)r.StatusCode} {await r.Content.ReadAsStringAsync()}");
Console.WriteLine($"handler invocations: {n}");
```

Actual output:
```
follower: 500
handler invocations: 1
```
Expected: `follower: 200 value 2`.

### Exceptions (if any)

`TaskCanceledException` from the leader's `Task.Delay(..., ctx.RequestAborted)` surfaces as an unhandled exception on the follower's request.

### .NET Version

11.0.100-rc.3.26522.148 (also reproduces on 9.0.7)

### Anything else?

A possible fix is to catch the cancellation around the locking `ScheduleAsync` call when the current request itself isn't aborted, and fall through to executing the request:

```csharp
OutputCacheEntry? cacheEntry;
try
{
    cacheEntry = await _requestDispatcher.ScheduleAsync(context.CacheKey, key => ExecuteResponseAsync());
}
catch (OperationCanceledException) when (!executed && !httpContext.RequestAborted.IsCancellationRequested)
{
    // The request producing the response was canceled, not this one: process it ourselves.
    cacheEntry = null;
}
```

Happy to send a PR with a test in `OutputCacheMiddlewareTests`.

